    "turn_text": "Welcome to Women's Media Center Live. I'm Robin Morgan. And, of course, there's the conversation. Look at it this way. Let's say you're 78 years old. You've had a distinguished career in public service. In fact, you've received the National Presidential Medal of Freedom, which is the highest civilian award that can be bestowed in this nation. And you know, as you go to sleep each night, that you have helped save the lives of over 7 million children. Pretty good feeling. And you've received all sorts of honors and awards, and you're proud of the work you've done, and you live in Florida, and everybody assumes, well, it's 78, you're going to retire. What do you do? You run for Congress, and you win. And that's my wonderful guest this week, a longtime friend, representative Donna Shalala, a newbie in Congress after having been a cabinet secretary, and it's a hell of a conversation. And there's of course the surrealism corner and there's fighting words and all of that's coming up but only after you and I go inside the headlines. By the time you hear this, Congressman and Chair of the House Judiciary Committee, Jerry Nadler, may well have issued a contempt citation, contempt of Congress to Attorney General Barr or a subpoena. daring him not to attend the hearing. God knows what will happen. So there's no use covering it because it'll break the second that I cover it. And I'm stressed and we're all very stressed here. And we really want to hear from Mueller and more about stress later. No, let's start with other news. Let's start with some good news this week. Yes. The Kansas Supreme Court has ruled that the state constitution protects abortion rights, blocking a 2015 state law that bans second trimester procedures. The ruling found that Kansas protections could potentially override any federal restrictions on the procedure which is a crucial finding that could pave the way for similar challenges in other states that are trying to strictly control abortion. As I mentioned recently, more than 11 states across the Midwest and South have been passing laws that ban procedures after six weeks, which is when many women don't even yet know that they're pregnant. Swarthmore College is up in arms over fraternities and ought to be. Dozens of students at Swathmore stormed fraternity houses after campus publications leaked details from a document in which members of the school's Phi Psi fraternity boasted about sexual conquest and proudly referenced sexual assaults. They referred to the rape attic and how getting girls up there and then getting them so drunk they would pass out facilitated sexual assault. Two fraternities at the school later announced they would voluntarily disband and the administration is investigating the situation, but women's groups and many parents are pressing for fraternities to be outlawed outright. The United Kingdom is trying a bizarre new tack in supposedly fighting crime, claiming it will help find perpetrators. If you're the victim of a crime and you report it, You must be prepared to turn over personal data from your mobile phone, laptop, tablet, or smartwatch. The police claim they have a duty to pursue all reasonable lines of inquiry, and these now exist in the devices of victims and witnesses, as well as suspects, particularly in cases where suspect and victim know each other. But this raises serious problems in sexual assault crimes, which are already underreported because victims fear being treated like the guilty parties. Privacy advocates say the police departments currently often improperly download cell phone data from people they detain without their knowledge or consent in this new zeal to follow digital trails. Under the new approach, victims and witnesses will routinely be asked to sign a form saying that they consent to the extra extracting of data from their electronic devices, which can mean text messages and emails, contacts, social media records, internet browsing history, and more. Otherwise, they're told the case might not proceed without this extra information. The group's victim assault support and rape crisis in England and Wales, an umbrella organization for rape crisis centers, said that the consent form was a misnomer because complainants lack a full and free choice as to whether to agree. The United Nations has backed a resolution on combating rape in conflict situations, but excluded references in the text to sexual and reproductive health after strong opposition from the United States. The vote was carried 13 in favor with Russia and China abstaining. The United States has threatened to veto the resolution, but this last minute concession got the Americans on board, as well as the additional omission of calls for a working group to review progress on ending sexual violence. The UK, France, and Belgium all expressed disappointment at the watered-down text. In recent months, the Trump regime has taken a really hard line, flatly refusing to agree to any UN documents that refer to sexual reproductive health on grounds that such language implies support for abortions. The Japanese imperial clan traces its lineage back to the seventh century, making it the world's oldest dynasty, if not monarchy, as well as to some people religion. For a very long time it was thought that the emperor was a god. But last week, the current emperor, Arkehito, resigned and abdicated, having gained permission from Parliament to do so via a law passed last year. The law also encouraged the government to study the possibility of allowing women born to the royal family to, no, no, no, no, no, no, not succeed to the throne, merely to remain within the imperial household after marrying. I mean, we're talking heavy duty patriarchy here. The Crown Prince, who's now the new emperor, Naruhito, age 59, is married to a commoner who twice refused his proposals because she did not want to give up her career in diplomacy. Still, it was a love match, and so Masako and Naruhito have one child. a daughter, Princess Aiko, age 17, not in line for the throne. Due to the declining ratio of male imperial members, the next in line would be Nohito's younger brother, and then his 83-year-old uncle, and his nephew, who is only 12. Paul's showed that the Japanese people would love to have an Empress, and actually rules barring women from heading lines of succession date back only to the nineteenth century in the Meiji era. Over the 126 generations of recorded emperors, eight women have ruled. Of course, there's always the consideration of doing away with the concept itself of an imperial family. And at this rate, the rather fanatic traditionalists might even let that happen, rather than staying their fake tradition by a female presence. Finally, an important health story. Yes, it's about stress. An increasing body of evidence shows that the time we spend on our smartphones is interfering with our sleep, self-esteem, relationships, memory, attention spans, creativity, productivity, problem-solving ability, and decision-making skills. Phew. This is science, folks. Now a new study shows that by chronically raising levels of cortisol in our bodies through stress hormones, our phones may also literally be shortening our lives. Until recently most discussions of the biochemical effects of smartphones focused on dopamine, the brain chemical that helps us form habits and addictions, because smartphones and apps, like slot machines, are actually designed to trigger dopamine release with the goal of making our devices harder to put down. Now it turns out that a phone's effect on cortisol is even more alarming than its effect on dopamine, since the hormone cortisol triggers physiological change such as spikes in blood pressure, heart rate, and blood sugar. Cortisol, like adrenaline, is a fight or flight hormone. It's triggered when we face danger or stress, and God knows we face danger and stress. The new study shows that our cortisol levels are elevated when the phone is in sight or nearby or when you hear it or even think you do. This study also found that in addition to long-term health consequences, smartphone-induced stress can threaten our lives in immediate ways. Elevated cortisol levels impair the prefrontal cortex, an area of the brain critical for decision-making and rational thoughts, as well as for self-control. So, for example, texting while driving, even while knowing that's a stupid suicidal thing to do. Medical health experts suggest making the phone less stressful by turning off all notifications except for those you actually need to receive. Also, try filtering your apps. Take those you find you check out of anxiety hide them in a folder off your home screen. You could also reduce the gimmicks on your phone and refocus it on being just a phone and then you could go for a walk in the park. Also scientifically shown to be a stress reducer proven. In other words create real-life algorithms and dance to those those beats which might actually lengthen your life. We have to take a short break here, but remember that the conversation with Representative Donna Shalala is still coming up. And before we do that, we're going to visit right away when we come back from this break, the surrealism corner. Subscribe to this program on your favorite podcast platform and leave us a review. Follow us on Twitter at WMC Live, like our Facebook page at Facebook.com slash WMC Live, and visit us at WMCLive.com for more information about our show and our guests.",
