    "turn_text": "We are back again and on our way to the surrealism corner. You must by now have heard of Stephen Moore, the right-wing pundit whose focus is on economic issues, He's the one who founded the Anti-Tax Club for Growth, and he is Trump's candidate to sit on the Federal Reserve Board, which is supposedly completely independent from the government. But lately Moore has upset even some Republicans with the sheer blatant misogyny of his remarks. I won't repeat and give airtime to them here except for one because actually I find it so heartening. He laments radical feminism taking over universities. In fact, he says, It is a well-established fact that radical feminism has taken over the culture, especially at the elite East Coast universities.\" He does go on and on, but the point here is that I didn't know we already have taken over in such a thorough manner. Isn't that terrific? Check off the universities and soon check off the culture itself. Now you and I can both sleep better at night assured that the earth will be safe what with radical feminists running things. Ah, breaking news, and it's good. Just as I was writing this, Moore has now withdrawn his name from nomination for the post. Betcha those radical feminists were behind that. now for some surreal short news bits. Oxford University researchers estimate that under a conservative scenario where no new users joined Facebook, which seems to be happening more and more, by the year 2100, well, by then 1.4 billion Facebook users will be dead. By 2070, the dead will outnumber the living MIT Technology Review commented that such startling truths about mortality highlight social networks dilemma of what to do with dead people. Indeed, you can't continue to sell their data, or can you? That after all is how Facebook makes its gazillions, we are the product. And even Amazon hasn't yet figured out an algorithm to keep dead people still buying stuff. Lisa Burnback, writing in The New Yorker, reported on the most hideous men in New York City walking tour, held by L Magazine's advice columnist, E. Jean Carroll. Carroll, who is 76 and takes no prisoners, warns at the tour's beginning, quote, if you feel that we are about to be unfair to men, get the hell out. Apparently, she then leads her charges to a number of sites like the Plaza Hotel where until 1969 in women demonstrating only men were allowed to eat lunch in the oak room. or Trump Tower, no comment or explanation is necessary. And St. Patrick's Cathedral and Sexual Predator Cardinal Theodore McCarrick, or Rockefeller Center, that's both Bill Cosby and Matt Lauer. The offices of Fox News, that's both Roger Ailes and Bill O'Reilly, and CBS headquarters on West 52nd Street, where Charlie Rose and Les Moonves committed their offenses. Carolyn might expand her hideous men tours, which now take place on the first and third Sundays of every month. Clearly, she knows there's plenty of turf for expansion. Here is a great surreal new discovery. Scientists have developed a virtual prosthetic voice, a system that decodes the brain's vocal interventions and translates them into mostly understandable speech with no need to move a single muscle. Thousands of people are reduced to painstaking means of communication, an eye blink, for example, as a result of injuries from accidents, combat, strokes, or neurodegenerative disorders like ALS, all of which can disable the ability to speak. Previously, it was necessary to have at least one muscle through which the brain could work. Stephen Hawking used a muscle in his cheek to type keyboard characters, for instance. But this new system decifers the brain's motor commands guiding vocal movement during speech and directly generates intelligible sentences that approximate the speaker's natural cadence. So you can be completely immobilized yet able to communicate. Thanks, science. This item deserves to be in a category titled, if our species can do this sort of miraculous stuff, Why, in hell, are we still waging wars? In a similar vein, far out but heartening, new research in the journal Cell found that there are some 200,000 species of virus in our planet's oceans at twelve times more than previously thought. The research could potentially help scientists engineer the ocean at some point to combat climate change. Go, science, go. last two notes on language. There I was absorbed in an article about how microbial life could be delivered between different planetary systems via rocky debris like asteroids or comets, and I suddenly stumbled and fell flat over an irritating word. You know how it is when you think you're safely reading about a totally non-sexist subject and yet you find somebody has egregiously managed to insert the patriarchy into it? Here was the quote. This scenario brings to mind the well-known theory of panspermia. If it can be proven that small, rocky objects can indeed travel between planetary systems, that would increase the chances of panspermia actually occurring, seeding the galaxy with life, even if perhaps only rarely. If the debris hit a planet with suitable conditions, then those microbes might find a happy new home.\" Now, sperm is a word from the Greek word for seed, but now it's associated, well, you know with what, and why they couldn't simply have used the word seed is beyond me. But also, notice the phrase, if the debris hit a planet with suitable conditions, because if not, forget about it. So how about resting the definition on that? Suitability, receptivity, fertility. For that matter, how about a word referring to a planet's active drawing in these floating seed microbes? Something like, I don't know, planetary magnetism? The other language item is, of course, our weekly word from another language that can only be approximated in English with an entire sentence. This week's word is from German, and it fits those of us who cringe in phantom pain when seeing others who seem wretchedly miserable. It's empathy, perhaps overdone. The word in German is from de shameen, You can experience from the shame and exterior shame for someone else when seeing them in an uncomfortable or embarrassing situation. For example, whenever I see Sarah Huckabee Sanders squirm before the world media while trying to explain and defend her boss Donald Trump, knowing as she in fact does that her own lies have to follow his. I actually find myself feeling friend Seema for her, and she's not even my friend. Time for another short break, and then coming right up is my conversation with a woman who At one point was called Madam Secretary when she was a cabinet member. And at another point was called President when she headed various major universities in this country. And she is a mean softball player. And now you address her as Congresswoman, Donna Shalala. The Women's Media Center, founded in 2005 by Jane Fonda, Gloria Steinem and myself, is a progressive, nonpartisan, nonprofit organization working to make women visible and powerful in media. You can learn more about the WMC at women'smediacenter.com.",
