    "turn_text": "Welcome back again. Today the conversation is with a beloved longtime friend who is now the proud representative in the House of Congress for Florida's 27th District which includes the City of Miami and surrounding municipalities in Miami-Dade County. Donna Shalala is the granddaughter of immigrants from Lebanon. She has served as president or chancellor of three major universities She's a member of the Council on Foreign Relations, and she began her career in public service as one of the country's first Peace Corps volunteers in Iran. She was appointed then in 1977 by President Jimmy Carter, who tapped her to serve as Assistant Secretary for Policy Development and Research at the U.S. Department of Housing and Urban Development. Then in 1993, She was nominated by President Bill Clinton to serve as Secretary of Health and Human Services. And there she created and implemented and oversaw CHIP. I know you've heard about CHIP a lot. The Children's Health Insurance Program of which Hillary Rodham Clinton is so proud. She worked on it together with Donna. It currently covers over 7.6 million children, and of course it's in danger from the Trump administration. Shalala also succeeded in doubling the budget of the National Institute of Health, and she secured the highest immunization rates in American history, particularly relevant to look back on that, given the resistance to inoculations today and the growing measles epidemic as a consequence. She served longer as head of HHS than anyone else ever has in American history. In 2007, President George Bush Handpicked her to co-chair with Senator Bob Dole, the Commission on Care for Returning Wounded Warriors, and in 2008, President Bush selected her as the recipient of the Presidential Medal of Freedom, the nation's highest civilian award. She's an unapologetic feminist and she's terrific. Here is the great Donna Shalala. Donna, Congresswoman, welcome to the program, Donna. Thank you. It's so amazing to have you here as a congresswoman.",
