  {
    "episode_id": 93268,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/3/93268.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 109,
    "turn_text": "a train. Welcome back to some fighting words. Rather than go bonkers over Attorney General William Barr, or the carnage in Venezuela spilling over onto the rest of the continent, or so many other choices for driving one up the wall and through the ceiling, I decided do something positive, small, good, positive. Not my style to be a chirpy Pollyanna, but rolling up one's sleeves and acting to change the way things are always makes me feel better, even if it has no immediately identifiable effect or even if it has no effect at all. So I worked for a bit with our superb president at the Women's Media Center, Julie Burton, about a new project. Now keep in mind that the WMC publishes regular reports like the annual Women in Media report, which is so invaluable that journalists now consider the Women's Media Center as the go-to place for analysis and statistics on sex and race and other subjects as well. We always try to add recommendations or guidelines at the back of all reports as a kind of, so what can we do about this section? And some reports are subject specific. For example, these are actual titles. The ten do's and don'ts on how to sensitively interview sexualized violence survivors, or writing rape, how journalists cover campus rape, or the gender gap in coverage of reproductive issues, and the Women's Media Center Guide to Covering Reproductive Issues. and then there's also name it change it the women's media center guide to gender neutral coverage of women candidates all of these titles by the way are available for free on the women's media center website which is just women's media center dot com you can download them and use them as you will But the new project we're working on is in keeping with today's very fast pace and somewhat public attention deficit disorder epidemic. It's swift, short, focused and packed with information. It was inspired by work the WMC itself has done in the area of guides for action and also inspired by a feminist organization in Britain called Level Up. Level Up recently won its campaign calling for the media to change the way it reports on fatal incidents of domestic violence. The UK's two leading press regulators, IPSO and Impress, are adopting the guidelines. That's in a bid to combat irresponsible reporting that exacerbates the trauma for families of domestic homicide victims. The guidelines written by Level Up Coalition of Academics, Survivors, Families, and Representatives from Domestic Violence in GEOs suggests that there are ways of reporting that are safe and sensitive. They offer five tips in the Level Up campaign, and these are now going to be adopted in Britain. Think of the difference these can make. 1. Accountability. Place responsibility solely on the killer, which means avoiding speculative, quote, reasons, or, quote, triggers, or describing the murder as an uncharacteristic event. You know, all of those. He was such a nice man. Homicides are usually underpinned by a long-standing sense of ownership, coercive control, and possessive behavior. They are not random events. 2. Accuracy. Name the crime as domestic violence instead of, quote, tragedy or, quote, horror and include the National Domestic Violence Helpline at the end of the article or news report. 3. Dignity. Avoid sensationalizing language and invasive or graphic details to compromise the dignity of the dead woman or her surviving family members. 4. Equality. Avoid insensitive or trivializing language or images. 5. Images themselves. Avoid using stock images that reinforce the myth that domestic violence is only a physical crime. All of the above constitute excellent advice, and I applaud Level Up, which was founded in 2016 by a group of feminists aiming to tackle sexism in the UK with education and activism. And I'm quite excited that there's going to be a series of WMC guides, one-pagers for journalists and civilians clarifying accurate language and attitude on a number of subjects. Sexual harassment at work, domestic violence, domestic homicide, campus sexist violence, well, on and on, you get the point. For me, personally, the misuse of language is a constant ongoing act of violence, with real consequences. When the anti-science friends of the fetus people refer to late-term abortion as, quote, partial birth abortion, that's a political act of violence against the woman involved and against the truth. For that matter, reporting abortion restrictions by referring to, quote, the life of the mother is a misnomer and a misleading. The phrase should be the life of the woman. It changed attitudes when, thanks to the women's movement, we all began to use the word survivor instead of victim when referring to sexual assault and domestic violence cases, unless, of course, the woman did not survive. Naturally, this honest reframing and reclaiming of consciousness through language is true not only in terms of sexism. For instance, for some time now, I've noted with concern that white people identify race only when talking about persons of color, yet we never or very rarely identify white people as such, as if white is the generic, a synonym for normal people. I don't believe in being colorblind, because in a racist society like ours colorblindness turns out to be a denial of racism. Rather, I think it would be good if we recognized what was going on by becoming color literate, and identifying white people as such is quite a consciousness-raising experience. You know, it took three days before I could manage to find out any information about the ethnicity of the latest California synagogue terrorist killer, who was white, male, young, and Christian. And had he been a Muslim or an African-American or any other person of color, we would have known that somehow we would have been told it immediately. All of these small changes aren't small. How we speak affects how we think and how we act. Acting differently in a climate rife with lying and chicanery is like being offered oxygen in the middle of a great smog. We'll keep you up to date on the development of the WMC guideline series. Now, it won't be ready for some months, but it is on the way, and it's just the kind of thing that we do. As for those Troglodytes who claim this is all PC, I dismissed them with what I said years ago that PC really should stand for plain courtesy. It could also stand for principled cognition. Those are my fighting words. Add yours on Twitter and remember to tag us at WMCLive and use the hashtag fighting words. And that's our show for today. You can follow me on Twitter at TheRobinMorgan and on Facebook at facebook.com slash TheRobinMorgan. Check out my blog at robinmorgan.net and find my latest book, Dark Matter, Wherever Books Are Sold. Opinions expressed on this program do not necessarily represent the Women's Media Center. Here's the family for our show. Executive producer as well as founding and sustaining sponsor Regina K. Scully, WMC president and co-executive producer of this program, Julie Burton. I'm Robin Morgan, your producer and host. Thank you for being with us. Join us again next time on Women's Media Center Live. you",
